# PUT_NP carrying Memory Read requests, answered with DEFER.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. See put_pc_memory_write32.espi for why
# every peripheral fixture has to be.
#
# Three things this pins that nothing else does.
#
# 1. A LENGTH OF ALL ZEROS IS 4 KB, NOT ZERO. Section 4.1.3, p.50: "The length
#    field is 1-based. A value of all zeros indicates 4 KB of length." Table 5
#    does not say this and no packet figure hints at it, so a decoder written
#    from the table alone prints "0 bytes" here -- wrong, and wrong in the one
#    case that carries the most data. A Memory Read request is where this can
#    be shown cheaply: the length is the size being *asked for*, so the field
#    is fully meaningful while the packet itself carries no payload.
#
# 2. DEFER CARRIES NO COMPLETION. Figures 24 and 25, pp.38-39, are the same
#    PUT_NP answered two ways -- `ACCEPT HDR DATA STS CRC` and `DEFER STS CRC`.
#    That is not a labelling difference: it changes how many bytes the response
#    phase holds, so a decoder that always expects a completion header puts the
#    CRC three bytes early and reports a good transaction as corrupt.
#
# 3. THE 64-BIT FORM HAS AN 11-BYTE HEADER, not 7. Figure 36, p.54.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).

# Memory Read 32 of a whole 4 KB page. Tag 0, Length 000h.
CMD 02 00 00 00 FE D4 00 00 65
TAR
RSP 01 0F 01 AF

# Memory Read 64 of 64 bytes at 1_0000_0000h -- the first address above 4 GB,
# which is where section 4.1.4 p.51 says the 64-bit form starts being legal.
CMD 02 02 00 40 00 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 FC
TAR
RSP 01 0F 01 AF

# The same command answered as a CONNECTED completion. Figure 24, p.38, draws
# PUT_NP turning into `ACCEPT HDR DATA STS CRC` -- so a PUT_NP can carry a
# header in both phases, and the response one is a completion answering the
# request the command just made.
#
# This is the transaction that makes the response-side cycle header observable.
# Every other PUT_NP here is deferred, and a shape that dropped the response
# header entirely would decode all of those perfectly.
#
#   02 00 30 04 FE D4 00 00   PUT_NP, Memory Read 32, tag 3, 4 bytes
#   08                        ACCEPT
#   0F 30 04 11 22 33 44      Successful Completion With Data, "only
#                             completion", tag 3, 4 bytes -- the tag matches
#                             the request, which is what section 4.1.2 p.50
#                             means by using Tag to pair the two.
CMD 02 00 30 04 FE D4 00 00 4F
TAR
RSP 08 0F 30 04 11 22 33 44 0F 01 A1

# The same request pointed below 4 GB. Section 4.1.4, p.51: "For addresses
# below 4 GB, the memory transactions must use the 32 bits addressing format.
# When 64 bits addressing format is used, the upper 32 bits address [63:32]
# must not be all 0." The transaction is well formed in every other respect
# and reaches the right address, so nothing else on the bus flags it -- the
# same shape of finding as a config address with its top four bits set.
CMD 02 02 00 40 00 00 00 00 FE D4 00 00 DD
TAR
RSP 01 0F 01 AF
